            "bug_id": 19203,
            "text": "?read.dcf documents that invalid UTF-8 bytes are \"preserved visually by escaping them as <xx>\". Since r90200, a well-formed 4-byte sequence for a code point above U+10FFFF (lead bytes F5 to F7, or F4 90 and up) instead comes back as \"????\", and the C reader re-encodes the whole accumulated field value for every further continuation line of that field. R 4.6.1 returns the raw bytes unchanged.\n\nReproducible example (R-devel r90642, macOS; same on Linux with glibc 2.39):\n\n    bad <- rawToChar(as.raw(c(0xf6, 0xb6, 0xb6, 0xb6)))\n    f <- function(x) { con <- textConnection(x); on.exit(close(con)); read.dcf(con) }\n    f(paste0(\"A: v\\n \", bad, \"\\n lue\\n\"))[[1L, \"A\"]]\n    ## observed:  \"v\\n????\\nlue\"\n    ## expected:  \"v\\n<f6><b6><b6><b6>\\nlue\"\n\n    for (n in c(5000L, 20000L)) {\n        x <- paste0(\"A: v\\n \", bad, strrep(\"\\n lue\", n), \"\\n\")\n        print(system.time(f(x))[[\"elapsed\"]])\n    }\n    ## observed:  0.84 s and 18.0 s      (R 4.6.1: 0.05 s and 0.86 s)\n\nAnalysis:\n\nmkCharUTF8sub() in src/main/dcf.c checks utf8Valid() and, when that fails, repairs the string through reEnc3() (iconv UTF-8 to UTF-8 with \"<xx>\" substitution). glibc and libiconv both accept the 4-byte sequence (as a code point up to U+1FFFFF) and pass it through, while utf8Valid() rejects it per RFC 3629, so the repaired value never satisfies the check. The continuation-line branch of do_readDCF() rebuilds the value as \"stored value + newline + line\" and calls mkCharUTF8sub() on the whole thing, so every further line goes through iconv() again. The R wrapper then sees that the result fails validUTF8() and applies .enc2utf8_sub(), whose ASCII fallback yields the \"????\".\n\nProposed patch: attached as readdcf-escape-invalid-utf8.diff (against r90642). It escapes invalid bytes using utf8Valid()'s own rules instead of iconv(): a small utf8ValidClen() helper next to utf8Valid() in src/main/util.c gives the length of the valid character at a position (or 0), and mkCharUTF8sub() copies valid characters and escapes every other byte as \"<xx>\". The result is valid by construction, so later lines take the fast path, and the escapes match those of iconv(sub = \"byte\") for everything it does reject. With the patch the example gives \"v\\n<f6><b6><b6><b6>\\nlue\" in 0.07 s and 1.1 s. A regression test is added to tests/reg-tests-1e.R.\n\nThe patch is independent of the one in bug 19194, which changes the same function for the field-name matching regression; both are fallout from r90200. The underlying discrepancy, that iconv(x, \"UTF-8\", \"UTF-8\", sub = \"byte\") can return a string validUTF8() rejects, is what the ASCII fallback in .enc2utf8_sub() exists for; this patch only addresses the C reader.\n\nThe patch is also submitted as https://github.com/r-devel/r-svn/pull/359, where CI results can be seen.",
